PLAN and BUILD STAGE 4/IV

Yggdrasil Stage 4 — Modular Library & Embedding Interface
Since GlacioJS will embed Yggdrasil, Stage 4 splits it from a CLI tool into a C library with a thin CLI wrapper. No CMake — just Make + shell scripts.
1. Architectural Split
plain
Copy

libyggdrasil.a / libyggdrasil.so   (core engine)
└── Lexer, Parser, AST, CodeGen, batch logic

yggdrasil CLI binary              (thin executable)
└── argc/argv parsing, file I/O, stdout, watch mode

The CLI becomes a user of the library, not the owner of the logic.
2. C API for GlacioJS Embedding
GlacioJS is C/C++, so expose a pure C interface. No STL, no exceptions, no C++ classes across the boundary.
c
Copy

// yggdrasil.h — public embedding API
#ifndef YGGDRASIL_H
#define YGGDRASIL_H

#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif

typedef struct ygg_config {
    const char* pragma;          // "React.createElement", "h", "_jsx"
    int runtime;                 // 0=classic, 1=automatic
    int no_copy;                 // skip non-JSX .js files in batch
    // GlacioJS can inject its own allocator
    void* (*alloc_fn)(size_t);
    void (*free_fn)(void*);
} ygg_config;

typedef struct ygg_result {
    int ok;                      // 0 = error
    char* output;                // owned by caller, must free
    char* error_msg;             // null if ok
    int error_line;
    int error_col;
} ygg_result;

// Lifecycle
void yggdrasil_init(void);       // global init (idempotent)
void yggdrasil_shutdown(void);

// Single-shot transpile (string in, string out)
ygg_result yggdrasil_transpile_string(const char* source, const ygg_config* cfg);

// File-based
ygg_result yggdrasil_transpile_file(const char* input_path, const ygg_config* cfg);

// Batch directory (returns array of results or writes to disk)
int yggdrasil_transpile_dir(const char* src_dir, const char* out_dir, const ygg_config* cfg);

// Error cleanup
void ygg_result_free(ygg_result* r);

#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
#endif

Key rule: GlacioJS calls yggdrasil_transpile_string(), gets JS back, owns the memory. Yggdrasil never touches GlacioJS internals.
3. Internal C++ ↔ C Boundary
Inside libyggdrasil, you write C++ as usual (classes, AST, RAII). But the public surface is a thin C shim:
cpp
Copy

// yggdrasil_c_api.cpp — glue layer
extern "C" ygg_result yggdrasil_transpile_string(const char* source, const ygg_config* cfg) {
    try {
        auto lexer = Lexer(source);
        auto parser = Parser(lexer);
        auto ast = parser.parse();
        auto codegen = CodeGen(ast, cfg->pragma);
        return {1, strdup(codegen.emit().c_str()), nullptr, 0, 0};
    } catch (const ParseError& e) {
        return {0, nullptr, strdup(e.what()), e.line, e.col};
    }
}

This keeps your C++ internals clean while giving GlacioJS a stable ABI.
4. Headers required for shareable code interface and working
6. Memory & Allocator Hooks
Since GlacioJS cares about explicit memory ownership, Yggdrasil should accept optional allocator callbacks:
c
Copy

typedef struct ygg_config {
    void* (*alloc)(size_t size);   // default: malloc
    void (*free)(void* ptr);       // default: free
    void* (*realloc)(void* ptr, size_t size); // default: realloc
} ygg_config;

Internally, all new/delete or malloc/free calls route through a single ygg_alloc() / ygg_free() indirection. If GlacioJS passes its own arena allocator, Yggdrasil lives inside that memory space. If null, Yggdrasil uses the CRT defaults.
7. Error Handling Contract

    No exceptions thrown across the C API boundary. All internal exceptions are caught in the C shim and converted to ygg_result error structs.
    GlacioJS must call ygg_result_free() on every ygg_result returned, even on success (to free the output string).
    Thread safety: Each yggdrasil_transpile_* call is stateless and reentrant. No global mutable state. Multiple GlacioJS isolates can call it concurrently without locks.

8. Versioning
c
Copy

#define YGGDRASIL_VERSION_MAJOR 1
#define YGGDRASIL_VERSION_MINOR 0
#define YGGDRASIL_VERSION_PATCH 0

const char* yggdrasil_version_string(void);
int yggdrasil_version_check(int major, int minor); // returns 1 if compatible

GlacioJS can compile-time check #if YGGDRASIL_VERSION_MAJOR >= 1.
9. What Stage 4 Is NOT

    ❌ No plugin system (not needed for V1)
    ❌ No dynamic library loading of transforms
    ❌ No CMake, no Meson, no Bazel (Make + scripts only)
    ❌ No networking, no file watcher inside the core lib (CLI handles watch mode, lib handles transpile logic)

Stage 4 Summary
Table
Deliverable	Description
libyggdrasil.a / .so / .lib	Static/shared library with all transpiler logic
include/yggdrasil.h	Single public C header
yggdrasil CLI	Thin wrapper, unchanged UX from Stage 3
Makefile	Linux/macOS build
build.sh / build.ps1	Cross-platform wrapper scripts
Allocator hooks	Optional custom malloc/free for GlacioJS integration
C++ → C shim	Clean boundary, no C++ types leaked
Effort: +1 week.
After Stage 4, GlacioJS can literally #include "yggdrasil.h" and call yggdrasil_transpile_string() to turn JSX into JS before feeding it to the QuickJS engine. Clean embedding, no process spawning, no CLI parsing inside GlacioJS.

